{
  "id": "hong_kong_quanzhen_daoist_ritual",
  "name": "Hong Kong Quanzhen Daoist ritual music (jingyun chant)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Ritual music of the Quanzhen Daoist temples of Hong Kong, such as Ching Chung Koon in Tuen Mun, Fung Ying Sin Koon and the Yuen Yuen Institute, studied by Tsao Poon-yee (Tsao Pen-yeh) of the Chinese University of Hong Kong from 1984. Scripture tunes (jingyun) are recited, intoned, chanted and sung by the Daoist masters, the second and third masters keeping the rhythm on the muyu woodblock and the qing bowl (the temple-bells card stands in for the qing). Ching Chung Koon and Fung Ying Sin Koon have modified traditional practice by adding a silk-and-bamboo ensemble of yangqin, huqin and pipa (the erhu card stands in for the huqin).",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1980s hong kong quanzhen daoist ritual music, ritual Devotional. metrical pitched chant fixed rhythm, group vocals, single lead single track no stacking. muyu, credited temple bells, traditional soft attack bamboo yangqin, bamboo stick horsehair threaded between strings erhu, pipa. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
